WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.989
Orchard Core can answer a question about your content in a single request.

00:00:05.989 --> 00:00:11.788
The GraphQL module turns your content types into a schema that clients query over HTTP.

00:00:11.788 --> 00:00:16.340
This video shows how it works, how to try it, and how to extend it.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:24.987
A page that lists posts needs a title and a path for each one.

00:00:24.987 --> 00:00:29.927
When the server fixes the shape of every response, the client either receives

00:00:29.927 --> 00:00:34.570
fields it never uses, or sends several requests to collect related data.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:39.873
With GraphQL, the client sends a query that names the fields it wants.

00:00:39.873 --> 00:00:44.245
The response has the same shape as the query, and related items are

00:00:44.245 --> 00:00:47.540
part of the same query, so one request is enough.

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:52.733
The GraphQL module builds the schema from your content types and parts.

00:00:52.733 --> 00:00:56.887
A content type you add appears in the schema without any code, and

00:00:56.887 --> 00:00:59.830
every request is checked against permissions.

00:01:04.081 --> 00:01:06.646
The GraphQL module is a feature.

00:01:06.646 --> 00:01:10.494
Let's open Tools, Features, and search for GraphQL.

00:01:10.494 --> 00:01:17.111
The Headless site recipe enables it during setup, and on other sites you enable it yourself.

00:01:17.565 --> 00:01:20.641
GraphQL is listed under the Api category.

00:01:20.641 --> 00:01:25.745
Select Enable, and a notification confirms that the feature was enabled.

00:01:26.211 --> 00:01:29.842
Tools has a GraphiQL entry once GraphQL is enabled.

00:01:29.842 --> 00:01:35.321
Modules such as Contents, Lists, Markdown and Autoroute each add their own types

00:01:35.321 --> 00:01:38.571
to the schema when they find GraphQL enabled.

00:01:42.779 --> 00:01:46.481
Tools, GraphiQL opens a page for writing and running queries.

00:01:46.481 --> 00:01:50.514
The Explorer on the left lists the fields you can query, the editor

00:01:50.514 --> 00:01:53.939
is in the middle, and the response appears on the right.

00:01:54.405 --> 00:01:58.288
Each content type is a field of the query, named after the type

00:01:58.288 --> 00:02:00.314
with a lowercase first letter.

00:02:00.314 --> 00:02:02.340
The BlogPost type is blogPost.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:06.335
This query asks for the display text of the published blog posts.

00:02:06.817 --> 00:02:08.012
Let's run it.

00:02:08.012 --> 00:02:11.093
The response is JSON, with a data property.

00:02:11.093 --> 00:02:15.747
Inside it, blogPost is a list with one entry for each published blog

00:02:15.747 --> 00:02:20.087
post, newest first, and each entry holds only the display text.

00:02:20.541 --> 00:02:23.270
One request can also follow relationships.

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:27.362
The list part of the blog holds its posts, and the inline fragment

00:02:27.362 --> 00:02:30.091
on BlogPost picks the fields of each post.

00:02:30.091 --> 00:02:33.501
Run it, and the blog and its posts come back together.

00:02:37.701 --> 00:02:42.178
A content type becomes a query field, unless its settings give it a

00:02:42.178 --> 00:02:44.202
stereotype, such as Widget.

00:02:44.202 --> 00:02:49.415
A part with fields or a GraphQL type becomes an object, unless it is collapsed.

00:02:49.415 --> 00:02:53.401
A part named like its type, such as BlogPost, is collapsed.

00:02:54.151 --> 00:02:57.525
Every content type query takes the same arguments.

00:02:57.525 --> 00:03:02.766
The where argument filters, orderBy sorts, first and skip page through the items,

00:03:02.766 --> 00:03:05.717
and status chooses which versions are read.

00:03:05.717 --> 00:03:09.151
The contentItem query returns one item from its id.

00:03:09.901 --> 00:03:12.943
The schema keeps up with your content definitions.

00:03:12.943 --> 00:03:17.288
After you change a content type, the next request builds the schema again,

00:03:17.288 --> 00:03:20.601
and GraphiQL shows the change when you reload the page.

00:03:24.801 --> 00:03:30.602
Outside the admin, any HTTP client calls the same endpoint, /api/graphql.

00:03:30.602 --> 00:03:35.154
A POST request sends the query in a JSON body, and a GET

00:03:35.154 --> 00:03:38.311
request sends it in the query string.

00:03:39.061 --> 00:03:44.719
A POST request can also use the application/graphql content type, where the body

00:03:44.719 --> 00:03:46.430
is the query itself.

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:51.233
Variables and the operation name travel in the JSON body, or in the

00:03:51.233 --> 00:03:53.601
query string of a GET request.

00:03:54.351 --> 00:03:56.115
The response is JSON.

00:03:56.115 --> 00:04:00.295
A successful query returns status 200 and a data property.

00:04:00.295 --> 00:04:05.521
When the query has errors, an errors list describes them, with status 400,

00:04:05.521 --> 00:04:09.571
or 401 when the user lacks a permission the query needs.

00:04:10.321 --> 00:04:13.814
Every request needs the Execute GraphQL permission.

00:04:13.814 --> 00:04:18.778
A client authenticates with the session cookie of a signed-in user, or with

00:04:18.778 --> 00:04:21.965
a bearer token from the OpenID Connect module.

00:04:21.965 --> 00:04:25.151
Anonymous users cannot run queries by default.

00:04:25.901 --> 00:04:28.179
Content permissions apply as well.

00:04:28.179 --> 00:04:32.621
A query returns only the content items that the user is allowed to view.

00:04:36.821 --> 00:04:42.242
The module reads its options from the configuration, under OrchardCore, Apis, GraphQL.

00:04:42.242 --> 00:04:46.367
They limit the depth and complexity of a query and the number of

00:04:46.367 --> 00:04:50.551
results, and they decide whether exception details reach clients.

00:04:54.751 --> 00:04:59.335
To expose your own part, write an ObjectGraphType that maps the fields you

00:04:59.335 --> 00:05:05.350
want, and register it with AddObjectGraphType in a startup class that requires the GraphQL feature.

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:08.501
The Autoroute module does this for AutoroutePart.

00:05:09.251 --> 00:05:13.313
For a query that no part provides, implement ISchemaBuilder.

00:05:13.313 --> 00:05:15.960
BuildAsync adds fields to the schema.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:21.499
GetIdentifierAsync returns a value that changes when the schema must be built again,

00:05:21.499 --> 00:05:25.191
or an empty string when nothing it depends on changes.

00:05:27.191 --> 00:05:29.065
That's the GraphQL module:

00:05:29.065 --> 00:05:34.510
a schema built from your content, one endpoint, and permission checks on every request.

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:38.199
The other videos of this series go deeper into each part.

00:05:38.199 --> 00:05:39.721
Thanks for watching.

